/* Histories for the four physics-D sheets: cosmo, astro, plasma, matsci. */
const HIST_16 = {

cosmo: {
 origin:[
  {y:'1917', who:'Einstein; de Sitter', t:'A constant invented to hold the universe still',
   b:'Einstein applies his field equations to the whole universe, assumes it is static because the fixed stars looked static, and finds no static solution. He adds Λ to manufacture one — and the manufactured solution is unstable, a fact Eddington pointed out only in 1930. Within months de Sitter produces a second solution: empty, with Λ alone, and with a redshift that grows with distance. So by the end of 1917 there were two cosmological models, one containing matter and no expansion, the other expansion and no matter, and nobody could see how to get both.'},
  {y:'1922–27', who:'Friedmann; Lemaître', t:'The expanding solutions, twice ignored',
   b:'Friedmann, a meteorologist in Petrograd, writes down the general non-static solutions in 1922. Einstein publishes a note claiming Friedmann has made an arithmetic error, then retracts it — the retraction\'s draft famously contained the word "suspicious" struck out and replaced by "clarifying". Friedmann died of typhoid in 1925. Lemaître, independently, derives the same solutions in 1927, connects them to Slipher\'s redshifts, and gets a rate of about 625 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹. Einstein told him the mathematics was correct and the physics abominable. The 1931 English translation of the paper omits the paragraphs deriving the rate — the excision was Lemaître\'s own, on the grounds that Hubble had by then done it better.'},
  {y:'1929', who:'Hubble; Slipher; Humason', t:'A bad plot that was right',
   b:'Hubble publishes velocity against distance for 24 nebulae and a slope of about 500 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹. Twenty of the velocities were Slipher\'s, from a 24-inch refractor at Flagstaff, and Slipher is not cited. The distances were wrong by a factor of about seven, because Hubble had conflated two kinds of Cepheid variable and had taken bright H II regions for individual stars. The scatter in the plot is comparable to the signal. It was nonetheless the right conclusion, and the discrepancy it created — a universe younger than the Earth — took until Baade\'s 1952 population correction to remove.'},
  {y:'1946–48', who:'Gamow; Alpher; Herman', t:'A hot beginning, and a prediction nobody kept',
   b:'Gamow argues that the elements were cooked in a hot dense early phase. Alpher and Herman work out that such a phase leaves relic radiation, now cooled to roughly 5 K. The nucleosynthesis programme fails at mass 5 and mass 8, where no stable nuclei exist, so the heavy elements had to come from somewhere else — and the relic-radiation prediction, being a by-product of a failed calculation, was simply forgotten. It played no part whatsoever in the 1965 detection.'},
  {y:'1948–65', who:'Bondi, Gold & Hoyle; Ryle', t:'The rival that was properly killed',
   b:'The steady-state model keeps the universe unchanging by creating matter at about one hydrogen atom per cubic metre per 10⁹ years. It was sharper than its opponent: it forbade evolution, so any observed change with lookback time refuted it. Ryle\'s Cambridge radio source counts in the 1950s found more faint sources than a non-evolving universe permits; quasars turned out to exist only at high redshift; and the microwave background has no natural thermalising mechanism in a steady state. Hoyle, who had coined "big bang" on the radio in 1949 as a term of dismissal, never conceded.'},
  {y:'1964–65', who:'Penzias & Wilson; Dicke, Peebles, Roll & Wilkinson', t:'Found by people who were not looking',
   b:'Penzias and Wilson at Bell Labs cannot get rid of 3.5 K of excess antenna noise in a 20-foot horn. They check the New York sky glow, the galactic plane, the seasons, and a pair of pigeons nesting in the throat, whose droppings they describe in print as a white dielectric material. Thirty miles away Dicke\'s group was building an antenna to look for exactly this. A phone call settled it, and the two papers ran back to back in the Astrophysical Journal, the discovery paper making no cosmological claim at all.'},
  {y:'1981–92', who:'Guth; Smoot and the COBE team', t:'Inflation, then the fluctuations it required',
   b:'Guth proposes an early epoch of accelerated expansion to explain why the universe is flat, uniform, and free of monopoles; his original version cannot end gracefully, and Linde and Albrecht–Steinhardt supply the repair within a year. The framework demands primordial fluctuations of order 10⁻⁵, and for a decade none were found — a real embarrassment, since without them there is no way to make galaxies. COBE\'s differential radiometer found them in 1992 at 7° resolution, ΔT/T ≈ 1.1×10⁻⁵.'},
  {y:'1998–2018', who:'Riess, Perlmutter, Schmidt; WMAP; Planck', t:'Acceleration, and six numbers',
   b:'Two teams set out to measure how fast the expansion is slowing and find it speeding up: distant type Ia supernovae are about 0.25 magnitudes fainter than a decelerating model allows. Λ, discarded in 1931, is restored — as a fitted number. WMAP and then Planck reduce the whole of cosmology to a six-parameter fit that reproduces some 2500 multipoles of the microwave sky. The model works superbly and explains nothing about what 95% of it is made of.'}
 ],
 exps:[
  {n:'Hubble\'s velocity–distance relation', who:'Edwin Hubble, Mount Wilson (velocities mostly V. M. Slipher, Lowell)', y:'1929', fig:'hubble100',
   setup:'Distances came from the 100-inch Hooker reflector on Mount Wilson: hours-long photographic exposures on glass plates, repeated over months to find Cepheid variables in the nearest spirals, with a period–luminosity calibration transferred from the Magellanic Clouds. Where no Cepheids could be resolved, Hubble used the apparent magnitude of what he took to be the brightest individual stars, and beyond that the total magnitude of the galaxy itself. Radial velocities were spectroscopic: for twenty of the twenty-four objects they came from Slipher\'s decade of work with a very fast camera on the 24-inch Lowell refractor, exposures running to 20–40 hours over several nights, measuring the Doppler displacement of the H and K lines of calcium.',
   meas:'Velocities from −220 km s⁻¹ (Andromeda, approaching) to +1090 km s⁻¹, plotted against distances out to about 2 Mpc on Hubble\'s scale. Least squares gave K = 465 ± 50 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹ from individual nebulae and 513 ± 60 from grouped ones; the quoted round figure was 500.',
   infer:'From a correlation in a sample of 24 to a global law of expansion. The inference is not deductive at any point: the scatter about the fitted line is of the same order as the velocities themselves, the relation is carried largely by the few most distant points, and a Copernican assumption is needed to convert "everything recedes from us" into "everything recedes from everything".',
   assume:'That the Cepheid period–luminosity relation is universal, that the brightest resolved objects in a galaxy are stars, and that redshift is a velocity. The first two were false: Baade showed in 1952 that Hubble had mixed classical Cepheids with the fainter W Virginis stars, and Sandage showed in 1958 that many of the brightest "stars" were H II regions. Together these inflated the slope by about a factor of seven. The uncontrollable assumption was that the observed motions are not dominated by local peculiar velocities — and at 2 Mpc, with a true expansion signal of ~140 km s⁻¹ and peculiar velocities of a few hundred, they largely are.'},
  {n:'The excess antenna temperature at 4080 Mc/s', who:'Arno Penzias & Robert Wilson, Bell Telephone Laboratories, Holmdel', y:'1964–65', fig:'hornant',
   setup:'A 20-foot horn-reflector antenna built for the Echo and Telstar satellite experiments, with a beam about 2° across and, crucially for this measurement, very low side-lobe response to the warm ground. The receiver was a ruby travelling-wave maser at 4080 MHz (7.35 cm). A Dicke switch alternated the input between the horn and a reference load — a section of waveguide immersed in liquid helium at 4.2 K, whose temperature was known absolutely from the helium vapour pressure. The instrument therefore measured a difference against a physical standard, not a calibration curve. The throat was disassembled, cleaned of pigeon droppings, and the aluminium joints taped, to eliminate the last suspected sources.',
   meas:'After subtracting 2.3 K of atmospheric emission, 0.9 K of ohmic loss in the horn and waveguide, and 0.1 K for the galactic background, an unexplained residual of 3.5 ± 1.0 K remained. It was isotropic to within the sensitivity, unpolarised, and showed no seasonal or diurnal variation.',
   infer:'A single number at a single frequency is not a blackbody. The inference to relic radiation from a hot early universe was supplied entirely by the companion paper from Dicke\'s Princeton group; Penzias and Wilson\'s own paper claims nothing beyond an unexplained excess. The blackbody character had to wait for measurements at other wavelengths, and the definitive one for twenty-five years.',
   assume:'That every terrestrial and instrumental contribution had been accounted for — the atmosphere, the ground, the horn walls, the receiver itself. Ed Ohm had measured a similar excess with the same antenna in 1961 and assigned it to the error budget, which is a good illustration of how much of this is a judgement about what counts as residual. The assumption they could not control was that the signal was not galactic: it took the absence of any concentration toward the plane, over a single year of observation with one beam, to rule that out.'},
  {n:'The COBE/FIRAS spectrum', who:'John Mather and the FIRAS team, NASA Goddard', y:'1990 (final calibration 2009)', fig:'firas',
   setup:'A polarising Michelson interferometer flown at 900 km, measuring the difference between the sky and an internal reference body rather than an absolute brightness. The whole optical assembly was held at about 1.5 K by superfluid helium so that the instrument itself radiated negligibly in band. The decisive component was the external calibrator: a re-entrant cone of absorber that could be swung into the aperture to fill the beam completely, with a measured emissivity better than 0.9999 and its own thermometry. To calibrate, the cone was placed in the beam and its temperature stepped until the interferogram nulled — so the measurement is a comparison of the sky against a laboratory blackbody carried into orbit.',
   meas:'Over roughly 60–600 GHz the sky spectrum fits a Planck function at T₀ = 2.72548 ± 0.00057 K, with residuals below 50 parts per million of the peak brightness. The first result, from nine minutes of data, was announced at the January 1990 AAS meeting with error bars smaller than the width of the plotted line.',
   infer:'A blackbody this good requires a period of thermal equilibrium between radiation and matter, and thereafter free expansion. No process operating in a cold universe is known that would thermalise starlight to this precision, which is the strongest single argument against steady-state and tired-light alternatives. The step that is not deductive is the "no known process" — it is an argument from the failure of a search, not a proof.',
   assume:'That the calibrator is a blackbody to the claimed precision, that the residual dust emission from our own Galaxy has been correctly modelled and subtracted at high latitude, and that the instrument is linear. The first was tested on the ground; the second is a fitted component and is the dominant systematic; the third was checked by varying the reference temperature. The assumption not fully controllable is that spectral distortions of the kinds now searched for (μ and y type) are not being absorbed into the fitted temperature, which is precisely what proposed successors to FIRAS are designed to test.'},
  {n:'The CMB angular power spectrum', who:'Planck Collaboration, ESA', y:'2009–2018', fig:null,
   setup:'A 1.5-m off-axis telescope at the second Lagrange point, scanning the full sky in nine bands from 30 to 857 GHz. The high-frequency instrument used 52 bolometers cooled to 100 mK by an open-cycle dilution refrigerator carrying its own helium-3 supply — the coldest sustained temperature ever operated in space, and a hard limit on the mission, which ended when the helium ran out after about thirty months. The spin axis was repointed so that each detector traced great circles, giving the redundancy needed to separate a genuine sky signal from 1/f detector drift.',
   meas:'The temperature and polarisation angular power spectra from ℓ = 2 to about 2500, with the first acoustic peak at ℓ₁ = 220.6 ± 0.6 and six further peaks resolved. Six free parameters fit the whole thing with χ² per degree of freedom near unity.',
   infer:'From a set of angular correlations to a physical model of a photon–baryon fluid oscillating in dark-matter wells. The inference runs through a Boltzmann code — CAMB or CLASS — that computes the predicted spectrum from the six parameters, so what is actually tested is the conjunction of the model, the recombination history, the assumed neutrino sector and the foreground model. Nothing here is read off the sky directly.',
   assume:'That the Galactic foregrounds — synchrotron, free–free, thermal and spinning dust, and unresolved point sources — can be separated using their different frequency dependences. This is why nine bands exist, and most of them exist only as foreground monitors. It is also the assumption that killed BICEP2\'s 2014 claim of primordial B-modes, which had too few frequencies to identify polarised dust. The uncontrollable one: the recombination calculation is taken from a code, and a one-percent error in it moves H₀ by more than the quoted uncertainty.'},
  {n:'Type Ia supernovae at z ≈ 0.5', who:'High-z Supernova Search Team (Riess, Schmidt) and Supernova Cosmology Project (Perlmutter)', y:'1998', fig:null,
   setup:'Wide-field CCD imaging of the same patches of sky just after new moon and again three weeks later, differenced to find transients, with follow-up spectroscopy on 4-m and 10-m telescopes to confirm the type and the redshift, then repeated photometry over weeks to build a light curve. The scheduling was the innovation: the searches were designed so that a guaranteed batch of candidates would exist on a pre-booked night at Keck. Light curves were corrected using the empirical fact that broader ones are brighter (the Phillips relation), which turns a class of objects with a 0.4-magnitude scatter into a standardisable candle with about 0.15.',
   meas:'Roughly fifty supernovae between z ≈ 0.3 and 0.9 appear about 0.25 magnitudes — some 12% — fainter than they would in a decelerating, matter-only universe.',
   infer:'Fainter means further, and further at a given redshift means the expansion accelerated. This requires a component with p < −ρc²/3. The inference is not deductive: the same faintness could come from evolution in the progenitors, or from grey dust. Both were addressed afterwards — by finding supernovae at z > 1, where the deceleration epoch makes them brighter again, which no simple dimming model reproduces.',
   assume:'That type Ia supernovae at z = 0.5 are drawn from the same population as those nearby, after light-curve correction; that extinction can be separated from cosmology using colours; that the Malmquist bias in a magnitude-limited search has been modelled. The one that could not be controlled at the time was progenitor evolution, since the progenitor system of a type Ia is still not identified. It is worth being blunt that both teams expected to measure a positive deceleration and that Λ was reinstated to fit the result, not to predict it.'}
 ],
 assume:[
  {a:'The universe is spatially homogeneous on scales above about 100 h⁻¹ Mpc.', k:'contested', n:'Isotropy about us is measured to 10⁻⁵; homogeneity is inferred by adding a Copernican premise. Sylos Labini and collaborators dispute that galaxy catalogues reach a homogeneity scale at all. If it fails the Friedmann equations do not apply and every parameter loses its meaning.'},
  {a:'Averaging the inhomogeneous universe and then applying the field equations gives the same answer as the reverse order.', k:'idealisation', n:'False in general, because the equations are non-linear. Buchert argues the backreaction can be percent-level or larger; Green and Wald have a theorem that under their hypotheses it cannot. This assumption is logically prior to every result in the model and is almost never listed.'},
  {a:'Dark matter is cold, collisionless and stable.', k:'empirical', n:'Supported by the peak structure, structure growth and cluster dynamics. "Cold" means non-relativistic at decoupling; warm candidates are constrained by the Lyman-α forest to m ≳ 5 keV. No laboratory detection in forty years of searching.'},
  {a:'Λ is constant in space and time, with w = −1 exactly.', k:'auxiliary', n:'Assumed, not measured to be so. DESI 2024–25 baryon-acoustic data prefer an evolving equation of state at 2.6σ–4.2σ depending on the supernova compilation used. If it goes, the model is replaced rather than adjusted.'},
  {a:'General relativity holds unmodified from solar-system scales to the horizon.', k:'auxiliary', n:'An extrapolation of fourteen orders of magnitude in length. GW170817 fixed the gravitational-wave speed to within 10⁻¹⁵ of light and eliminated a large class of alternatives, but the extrapolation itself remains an assumption, not a test.'},
  {a:'The primordial fluctuations are adiabatic, Gaussian and described by a power law.', k:'idealisation', n:'Two numbers stand in for an unknown pre-hot-phase history. Isocurvature modes are bounded to a few percent; local non-Gaussianity to f_NL = −0.9 ± 5.1. Any of these could have come out otherwise and would have changed the model rather than refuted it.'},
  {a:'Atomic and nuclear constants take their laboratory values at all redshifts.', k:'definition', n:'Every distance and every abundance is read off an assumed atomic physics. Quasar absorption spectra bound Δα/α below about 10⁻⁵ to z ≈ 4. Dropping it dissolves most of the evidence base rather than modifying it.'},
  {a:'The sound horizon at last scattering, r_d ≈ 147 Mpc, is computed correctly from the model.', k:'auxiliary', n:'It is the calibration of both the CMB and the BAO rulers, and it is not independently measured. The entire H₀ tension can be rewritten as a disagreement about r_d, which is exactly what early-dark-energy proposals exploit.'},
  {a:'The neutrino mass sum is 0.06 eV and the effective number of species is 3.046.', k:'auxiliary', n:'Fixed rather than fitted in the base six-parameter model, because the data cannot constrain them independently of other parameters. A fixed value quietly doing inferential work, and one that the same data are then said to constrain.'},
  {a:'Type Ia supernovae, Cepheids and the BAO feature are standard within their calibrations.', k:'empirical', n:'Each is a correspondence rule with its own chain of assumptions. The 5σ H₀ discrepancy is a disagreement between two such chains, not between two theories, which is why it has been so hard to adjudicate.'}
 ],
 notation:[
  {s:R`a(t),\ 1+z=1/a`, r:'"the scale factor"; "one plus zed"', m:'The single dynamical function of the background model, normalised to a(t₀) = 1 today. Redshift is defined by it, so z is a geometrical statement about how much the universe expanded while the light travelled, not a Doppler shift and not a velocity — the common "galaxies moving through space" gloss is wrong in this formalism.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`H=\dot a/a,\ \ H_{0}=100h\ \mathrm{km\,s^{-1}Mpc^{-1}}`, r:'"the Hubble parameter"; "aitch nought"', m:'The instantaneous fractional expansion rate. The factor h exists so that results can be quoted independently of the disputed value of H₀; h ≈ 0.67 from the CMB and ≈ 0.73 from the local ladder. 1/H₀ ≈ 14.5 Gyr, close to but not equal to the age.', d:'s⁻¹, quoted in km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹'},
  {s:R`\Omega_{i}=\rho_{i}/\rho_{c},\quad \rho_{c}=3H^{2}/8\pi G`, r:'"omega sub eye"; "the critical density"', m:'Densities in units of the value that makes space flat. ρ_c today is 8.5×10⁻²⁷ kg m⁻³, about five hydrogen atoms per cubic metre. The Ωs sum with Ω_k to exactly one by construction, so the flatness result is the statement that Ω_k is consistent with zero, not that the Ωs add up.', d:'dimensionless; kg m⁻³'},
  {s:R`k,\ \Omega_{k}=-kc^{2}/a^{2}H^{2}`, r:'"the curvature constant"', m:'k ∈ {−1,0,+1} after rescaling, giving open, flat or closed spatial slices. Ω_k = 0.0007 ± 0.0019 from Planck combined with BAO. Note that flatness is imposed in the base fit rather than measured within it; relaxing it costs a factor of several in the H₀ uncertainty.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\Lambda,\ \ w=p/\rho c^{2}`, r:'"lambda"; "double-you"', m:'Λ is a constant in the field equations with dimensions of inverse length squared; w is the equation-of-state parameter of a component, 0 for pressureless matter, 1/3 for radiation, −1 for a cosmological constant. Acceleration requires w < −1/3. Measured: w = −1.03 ± 0.03, assumed constant.', d:'m⁻²; dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\theta_{*}=r_{s}(z_{*})/D_{A}(z_{*})`, r:'"theta star"', m:'The angle subtended today by the sound horizon at last scattering: 100θ_* = 1.04109 ± 0.00030, the best-measured quantity in cosmology at 0.03%. It is a ratio of two computed lengths, so its precision is the model\'s precision — and its interpretation is what the H₀ tension is really about.', d:'radian'},
  {s:R`\ell,\ C_{\ell}`, r:'"ell"; "see sub ell"', m:'Multipole index of a spherical-harmonic expansion of the sky, with ℓ ≈ 180°/θ. The first acoustic peak sits at ℓ₁ = 220.6, i.e. about 0.8°. C_ℓ is the variance of the coefficients at that ℓ, and at low ℓ its own sampling error — cosmic variance — is irreducible because there is only one sky.', d:'dimensionless; K²'},
  {s:R`\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{R}}(k)=A_{s}(k/k_{*})^{n_{s}-1}`, r:'"the primordial power spectrum"', m:'The variance per logarithmic interval of the comoving curvature perturbation laid down before the hot phase, pivoted at k_* = 0.05 Mpc⁻¹. n_s = 1 would be exactly scale-invariant; the measured 0.9649 ± 0.0042 is a five-sigma departure and is the one quantitative inflationary success that was stated in advance.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\delta=\delta\rho/\bar\rho,\ \ \mathcal{H}=aH`, r:'"delta"; "script aitch"', m:'The fractional density contrast and the conformal-time Hubble rate. δ grows as a in the matter era — power-law, not exponential, because expansion drains gravitational instability. This is why 10⁻⁵ at recombination needs collisionless matter to reach order unity today.', d:'dimensionless; Mpc⁻¹ in conformal time'},
  {s:R`\tau,\ z_{*}\approx1090`, r:'"tau"; "zed star"', m:'Optical depth to Thomson scattering since reionisation, τ = 0.054 ± 0.007, and the redshift of last scattering. τ damps the small-scale anisotropies by e^{−2τ} and is degenerate with the amplitude A_s, which is why the combination A_s e^{−2τ} is measured far better than either.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\eta=n_{b}/n_{\gamma},\ \ Y_{p},\ \mathrm{D/H}`, r:'"eta"; "why sub pee"', m:'The baryon-to-photon ratio, about 6.1×10⁻¹⁰, and the primordial helium mass fraction and deuterium abundance it determines. η is the single free parameter of big-bang nucleosynthesis, which is why the agreement between the deuterium and CMB determinations is a genuine cross-check and not a fit.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`q_{0}=-\ddot a a/\dot a^{2},\ \ \sigma_{8},\ S_{8}=\sigma_{8}\sqrt{\Omega_{m}/0.3}`, r:'"cue nought"; "sigma eight"', m:'The deceleration parameter, defined with a minus sign in 1950s hopes that it would be positive; it is about −0.53. σ₈ is the rms density contrast in spheres of 8 h⁻¹ Mpc, and S₈ the combination weak lensing actually constrains, where Planck gives 0.832 ± 0.013 and lensing surveys 0.76–0.78.', d:'dimensionless'}
 ],
 worked:{t:'One baryon per 1.6 billion photons',
  steps:[
   'The photon number density of a blackbody is n_γ = (2ζ(3)/π²)(k_BT/ħc)³. At T₀ = 2.7255 K this gives n_γ = 410.7 photons per cubic centimetre — a number fixed by the FIRAS temperature alone, with no cosmological model in it.',
   'The critical density is ρ_c = 3H₀²/8πG = 1.8788×10⁻²⁶ h² kg m⁻³. The CMB acoustic peaks give Ω_b h² = 0.02237 ± 0.00015, so the baryon density is ρ_b = 0.02237 × 1.8788×10⁻²⁶ = 4.203×10⁻²⁸ kg m⁻³, and h cancels.',
   'Dividing by the mean mass per baryon, 1.673×10⁻²⁷ kg, gives n_b = 0.251 baryons per cubic metre, or 2.51×10⁻⁷ per cubic centimetre.',
   'Hence η = n_b/n_γ = 2.51×10⁻⁷ / 410.7 = 6.12×10⁻¹⁰. Both numerator and denominator scale as a⁻³ after electron–positron annihilation, so η is constant back to the first second.',
   'Now run the independent check. Deuterium measured in metal-poor damped Lyman-α absorbers gives D/H = (2.527 ± 0.030)×10⁻⁵, and the nucleosynthesis network converts that to η = 6.10 ± 0.08 ×10⁻¹⁰.'],
  a:'η ≈ 6.1×10⁻¹⁰: roughly one baryon for every 1.6 billion photons, agreed to better than 2% by a measurement of a spectral line in a quasar sightline at z ≈ 3 and a measurement of the angular positions of peaks in a microwave map. The two determinations share no instrument, no epoch, no wavelength band and almost no physics — nucleosynthesis at T ≈ 10⁹ K versus acoustic oscillation at T ≈ 3000 K. This concordance, not the fit quality of either alone, is what makes the hot big bang hard to doubt. It also raises the question the model cannot answer: a universe with equal matter and antimatter would have η ≈ 10⁻¹⁸, so the observed value records a baryon asymmetry of about one part in a billion that nothing in the Standard Model produces.'}
},

astro: {
 origin:[
  {y:'1854–99', who:'Helmholtz; Kelvin', t:'Gravitational contraction, and twenty million years',
   b:'If the Sun shines by slowly contracting, its energy supply is its own gravitational binding energy, GM²/R ≈ 4×10⁴¹ J, which at the observed luminosity lasts about 3×10⁷ years. Kelvin used this repeatedly against Darwin and against Lyell\'s uniformitarian geology, and he was right about the physics he had. The geologists, who had no mechanism and only stratigraphy, were right about the answer. It is the cleanest historical case of a rigorous quantitative argument from a more fundamental science being defeated by a discovery neither side could have anticipated.'},
  {y:'1920', who:'Eddington; Aston', t:'The crucibles of the stars',
   b:'Aston measures the mass of the helium nucleus and finds it 0.7% lighter than four hydrogen atoms. In his 1920 British Association address Eddington puts the two together: if 5% of a star\'s hydrogen became helium the energy would suffice, and "the stars are the crucibles in which the lighter atoms which abound in the nebulae are compounded into more complex elements." The objection was immediate and correct on its own terms — at a few million kelvin, protons cannot climb the Coulomb barrier. Eddington\'s reply, that the critics should "go and find a hotter place", was rhetoric, and he had no answer until Gamow supplied tunnelling in 1928.'},
  {y:'1924–26', who:'Eddington', t:'Luminosity without a source',
   b:'Eddington derives L ∝ M³ from hydrostatic equilibrium, radiative transport and the ideal gas law, with no nuclear physics whatever. He expected the relation to apply only to giants, believing dwarf interiors too dense to behave as perfect gases. When the dwarfs fell on the same curve he drew the right conclusion: ionisation strips the atoms, so stellar material remains a nearly ideal gas even at a hundred times the density of water. *The Internal Constitution of the Stars* (1926) is the founding text, and its central result was obtained in ignorance of the energy source.'},
  {y:'1925', who:'Cecilia Payne; Henry Norris Russell', t:'Stars are made of hydrogen, and the thesis says otherwise',
   b:'Payne applies Saha\'s ionisation theory to Harvard\'s objective-prism spectra and finds that the strength of a line measures excitation and ionisation far more than abundance. Correcting for this, hydrogen and helium come out roughly a million times more abundant by number than everything else. Russell, who had sent her the problem, wrote that the result was "clearly impossible"; the thesis states that the hydrogen and helium abundances are "almost certainly not real". Russell reached the same conclusion by a different route in 1929 and credited her, but the credit did not stick, and a correct result was published with a disclaimer attached by its author under pressure from a referee.'},
  {y:'1928–39', who:'Gamow; Atkinson & Houtermans; Bethe; von Weizsäcker', t:'Tunnelling, then the cycles',
   b:'Gamow explains alpha decay by barrier penetration; Atkinson and Houtermans immediately see that the same mechanism lets protons fuse at stellar temperatures. Bethe, at a Washington conference in 1938 convened specifically to get physicists and astronomers into a room, works out the proton–proton chain and the CNO cycle; von Weizsäcker has the CNO cycle independently. Bethe\'s 1939 paper is the one that closed the question, and he did not bother to publish it until a colleague pointed out it was eligible for a prize.'},
  {y:'1930–35', who:'Chandrasekhar; Eddington', t:'A limit, and a public humiliation',
   b:'Chandrasekhar, nineteen, computes on the voyage from Madras that relativistic degeneracy gives a maximum mass for a cold star, about 1.4 M⊙. At the Royal Astronomical Society in January 1935 Eddington followed Chandrasekhar\'s presentation by tearing up his paper on the podium and declaring that "there should be a law of Nature to prevent a star from behaving in this absurd way." Eddington\'s objection was to the relativistic degeneracy formula itself and was simply wrong; Dirac, Bohr and Pauli privately agreed with Chandrasekhar and none said so in public. Chandrasekhar left the field for a decade. The Nobel came in 1983.'},
  {y:'1946–57', who:'Hoyle; Burbidge, Burbidge, Fowler & Hoyle; Cameron', t:'Where the elements come from',
   b:'Gamow\'s proposal that all elements formed in the first minutes fails at mass 5 and 8, where no stable nuclei exist. Hoyle argues the synthesis happens in stars and, in 1953, predicts a resonance in carbon-12 near 7.7 MeV on the grounds that otherwise there would be no carbon; Fowler\'s Kellogg group found it at 7.68 MeV within months. B²FH in 1957 is 108 pages assigning every element to a process — hydrogen burning, helium burning, α, e, s, r, p, x — and it remains the framework. Fowler received the Nobel; Hoyle did not.'},
  {y:'1968–2002', who:'Davis; Bahcall; the SNO collaboration', t:'Thirty years of being told your star is wrong',
   b:'Davis\'s chlorine tank measures about a third of the neutrino rate Bahcall\'s solar models predict. Because the ⁸B flux scales roughly as the eighteenth power of the central temperature, a 5% error in the model would explain everything, and most physicists concluded the astrophysics was at fault. Bahcall refused, on the grounds that helioseismology pinned the central temperature far more tightly than that. SNO settled it by measuring the same neutrinos through a flavour-blind channel: the total flux matched the model to 1% and the electron neutrinos had oscillated. The theory that failed was particle physics.'},
  {y:'1975–2005', who:'Deubner; Christensen-Dalsgaard; Asplund', t:'The interior measured, and then unsettled',
   b:'Deubner resolves the ridges in the solar (ℓ,ν) diagram in 1975, converting a five-minute surface oscillation into a probe of the interior. Inversions of some 10⁵ mode frequencies give the sound speed from 0.05 to 0.95 R⊙ and agree with pre-existing models to a few parts per thousand. Then Asplund and colleagues redo the surface abundances in three dimensions and out of local thermodynamic equilibrium, lower the oxygen and carbon by about 30%, and the agreement breaks. Better spectroscopy produced a worse model, and twenty years on nobody knows which input is wrong.'}
 ],
 exps:[
  {n:'The mass–luminosity relation from binaries', who:'Arthur Eddington, Cambridge', y:'1924', fig:null,
   setup:'No apparatus was built: the measurement is an assembly of other people\'s astrometry and spectroscopy. Masses come from binary systems where Kepler\'s third law can be applied — visual binaries with a measured angular orbit and a trigonometric parallax, and spectroscopic binaries where the Doppler amplitudes give the mass ratio and, in eclipsing systems, the inclination too. Luminosities come from apparent magnitudes with the same parallaxes, plus bolometric corrections estimated from spectral type. Eddington had of order two dozen systems, spanning roughly 0.2 to 20 solar masses.',
   meas:'Luminosity rises as about the 3.5 power of mass across three decades of mass, with a scatter of order a factor of two in the original compilation and about 10% in modern eclipsing-binary samples.',
   infer:'The theoretical relation L ∝ μ⁴M³/κ follows from hydrostatic equilibrium, radiative diffusion and the ideal gas law with no reference to the energy source. Finding it obeyed therefore confirms the transport picture, not any nuclear hypothesis. Eddington had predicted the relation would fail for dwarfs, whose interiors he thought too dense to be ideal; that it held for them too is what told him stellar material is fully ionised.',
   assume:'That the parallaxes are right — the largest error source before Hipparcos — that the bolometric corrections are right, and that the stars are single, unevolved and chemically similar. The assumption that could not be controlled at the time was the composition: μ enters to the fourth power, and in 1924 hydrogen was not yet believed to be the dominant constituent, so the absolute normalisation of Eddington\'s relation was wrong even though its slope was right.'},
  {n:'Payne\'s abundance analysis', who:'Cecilia Payne, Harvard College Observatory', y:'1925', fig:'objprism',
   setup:'The data are Harvard objective-prism plates: a large prism mounted in front of the telescope aperture so that every star in the field records as a small spectrum, allowing tens of thousands of stellar spectra to be catalogued photographically. Line intensities had been estimated by eye on a coarse scale by the Harvard computers over three decades. Payne\'s contribution was the physics: she applied Saha\'s 1920 ionisation equation, calibrated against the marginal appearance and disappearance of lines along the spectral sequence, to work out the temperature and electron pressure at which each line reaches its maximum strength.',
   meas:'At the temperature where a line peaks, the fractional population of the responsible ionisation and excitation state is known, so the observed strength can be converted to an abundance. Hydrogen and helium came out roughly 10⁶ times more abundant by number than silicon, carbon or iron; the metals agreed with terrestrial proportions.',
   infer:'From line strength to abundance through a model of the atmosphere. The non-deductive steps are large: that the atmosphere is in local thermodynamic equilibrium, that a curve-of-growth treatment of saturated lines is adequate — hydrogen Balmer lines are heavily saturated and pressure-broadened, which is exactly where the treatment is weakest — and that the eye-estimated intensities are on a consistent scale.',
   assume:'Local thermodynamic equilibrium, a single representative temperature and pressure per star, and the completeness of the atomic data. The controls available were internal: the method had to reproduce the known terrestrial ratios among the metals, and it did. The uncontrolled assumption was the saturation correction for hydrogen, which is why the disclaimer she was pressed into adding was not wholly unreasonable as caution — only as conclusion. The result is right and the stated uncertainty was, if anything, honest.'},
  {n:'The Homestake chlorine experiment', who:'Raymond Davis Jr., Homestake Gold Mine, Lead, South Dakota', y:'1968–1994', fig:'homestake',
   setup:'A horizontal steel tank 6.1 m in diameter and 14.6 m long, holding 615 tonnes — about 380 000 litres — of tetrachloroethylene, ordinary dry-cleaning fluid, chosen because chlorine-37 is 24% of natural chlorine and the compound is cheap and dense in it. The tank sits on the 4850-foot level of a working gold mine, 1478 m of rock giving roughly 4200 metres water equivalent of shielding against cosmic-ray muons. A solar neutrino above 0.814 MeV converts ³⁷Cl to ³⁷Ar, which is a noble gas and comes out of solution. Every few weeks helium is bubbled through the whole tank to sweep out the argon, which is trapped on charcoal at liquid-nitrogen temperature, purified, and counted in a proportional counter of about half a cubic centimetre, using pulse rise time to distinguish the ³⁷Ar Auger-electron signature from background.',
   meas:'Argon production of about 0.48 atoms per day against a predicted 1.5. Roughly seventeen atoms of argon are extracted per run, of which about half are the signal. Averaged over 25 years, the capture rate is 2.56 ± 0.16 (stat) ± 0.16 (sys) solar neutrino units, against a standard-solar-model prediction near 8 SNU.',
   infer:'From a counting rate to a statement about the solar interior. Every link is an auxiliary: the ³⁷Cl absorption cross-section including transitions to excited states, the extraction efficiency for a handful of atoms in 380 tonnes, the counter efficiency, the cosmic-ray-induced background, and the predicted ⁸B flux, which depends on a nuclear cross-section extrapolated from laboratory energies. A factor-of-three deficit does not by itself say which link is broken.',
   assume:'That the argon extraction is quantitative — checked by spiking the tank with a known number of ³⁶Ar or ³⁸Ar atoms and recovering them, which Davis did on every run, obtaining efficiencies above 90%. That the residual background is understood — checked by measuring the muon-induced production at different depths. The assumption that could not be controlled was that neutrinos arrive as the flavour they left with; it was universally held, it was false, and it was the answer.'},
  {n:'The Sudbury Neutrino Observatory', who:'SNO Collaboration, Creighton Mine, Ontario', y:'1999–2006', fig:'snodet',
   setup:'One thousand tonnes of heavy water, on loan from Atomic Energy of Canada, in a transparent acrylic sphere 12 m across and 5 cm thick, suspended in a cavity 2092 m underground and surrounded by 7000 tonnes of ultrapure light water for shielding. A geodesic sphere 17.8 m in diameter carries 9456 photomultipliers of 20 cm aperture watching for Cherenkov light. The point of heavy water is that deuterium gives three distinct channels: a charged-current reaction sensitive only to electron neutrinos, a neutral-current reaction that breaks the deuteron and is equally sensitive to all three flavours, and elastic scattering off electrons with intermediate sensitivity. The neutron from the neutral-current channel was detected in three independent ways in successive phases — capture on deuterium, on dissolved salt, and in ³He proportional counters — precisely so that the key result would not rest on one technique.',
   meas:'The neutral-current flux is 5.09 (+0.44 −0.43) ×10⁶ cm⁻² s⁻¹; the charged-current flux is about a third of it. The standard solar model predicted 5.05 (+1.01 −0.81) ×10⁶.',
   infer:'The total flux of all flavours matches the astrophysical prediction to about 1%, while the electron-neutrino component is deficient. Two thirds of the electron neutrinos have therefore changed flavour in transit, which requires a non-zero neutrino mass. The inference is close to deductive given the cross-sections, which is what made it decisive where thirty years of chlorine and gallium results had not been.',
   assume:'That the neutral-current cross-section on deuterium is correctly calculated — it is, from effective field theory, to about 1–2% — and that the neutron detection efficiency is known, which is why three methods were used. That radioactive backgrounds from thorium and uranium in the acrylic and the water are below the neutron signal, controlled by continuous assay to parts in 10¹⁵ by mass. The residual assumption is that no exotic process other than oscillation converts flavours, which the measured energy dependence subsequently constrained.'},
  {n:'Helioseismic inversion for the sound speed', who:'BiSON, GONG and SOHO/MDI', y:'1975–', fig:null,
   setup:'Two complementary arrangements. BiSON uses six small stations around the world, each measuring the Doppler shift of the potassium D line integrated over the whole solar disc with a resonant-scattering cell, so that the Sun is observed as an unresolved star; this gives exquisite precision on low-degree modes that penetrate to the core. GONG and SOHO/MDI instead image the disc and measure the velocity field pixel by pixel, resolving modes up to ℓ ≈ 1000 that are trapped near the surface. Continuous coverage matters more than aperture: a gap in the time series produces sidelobes in the power spectrum that alias one mode onto another, which is why the network is global and why MDI was flown to L1.',
   meas:'Of order 10⁵ individual mode frequencies determined to a few parts per million, forming ridges in the wavenumber–frequency diagram. Each mode samples a different depth range, and the set is inverted for the sound speed profile c(r) and the internal rotation Ω(r,θ).',
   infer:'An inverse problem, not a measurement: the frequencies are integrals of c(r) along ray paths, and the inversion requires regularisation, which introduces a resolution kernel and a trade-off between resolution and error. What the result actually reports is a smoothed sound speed whose smoothing is a choice. Within that caveat, the agreement with models built before the data existed is better than 0.4% rms from 0.05 to 0.95 R⊙, and the convection-zone base is located at 0.713 ± 0.001 R⊙.',
   assume:'That the oscillations are linear, adiabatic and globally coherent; that the surface layers, where the adiabatic approximation fails and convection is not modelled, can be handled by an empirical "surface term" fitted as a smooth function of frequency. That last one is a fudge sitting directly on top of the most precise measurement in stellar astrophysics, and it is the reason absolute mode frequencies cannot be used, only differences and ratios.'}
 ],
 assume:[
  {a:'A star is spherically symmetric, non-rotating and non-magnetic.', k:'idealisation', n:'Excellent for the Sun, whose oblateness is about 10⁻⁵. Hopeless for Achernar, flattened by roughly a third, and for magnetically active M dwarfs whose radii exceed models by 5–10%. The one-dimensional reduction is what makes the calculation possible at all.'},
  {a:'Hydrostatic equilibrium holds at every instant.', k:'idealisation', n:'Justified by a 14-order-of-magnitude separation between the dynamical time (27 minutes for the Sun), the thermal time (3×10⁷ yr) and the nuclear time (10¹⁰ yr). It is abandoned outright in collapse, pulsation and thermonuclear runaway — that is, in every event a star is famous for.'},
  {a:'Matter is in local thermodynamic equilibrium.', k:'idealisation', n:'The photon mean free path in the solar interior is about 1 cm against a scale height of 10⁷ cm. It fails in the photosphere, and that failure is where the current solar abundance problem comes from: the revised oxygen abundance is a non-LTE, three-dimensional result.'},
  {a:'Convection can be represented by blobs travelling α_MLT pressure scale heights.', k:'auxiliary', n:'An admitted fudge. α is calibrated by forcing a solar model to reproduce the Sun\'s radius at its known age, then exported unchanged to other masses and metallicities. Three-dimensional simulations show the effective α varies by tens of percent across the HR diagram.'},
  {a:'Nuclear reaction rates measured at 0.1–1 MeV extrapolate to the 5–30 keV Gamow window.', k:'auxiliary', n:'The extrapolation spans a region where cross-sections are of order 10⁻¹² barn and cannot be measured except underground. It is the dominant uncertainty in predicted neutrino fluxes, which is why the LUNA accelerator was built under the Gran Sasso.'},
  {a:'Opacity tables computed from atomic structure give the true photon mean free path.', k:'contested', n:'The hinge of the mass–luminosity relation. The 2015 Sandia Z-machine measurement of iron opacity at conditions near the convection-zone base found values 30–400% above the tabulated ones, and the discrepancy is unexplained. If the tables are wrong, so is every stellar age.'},
  {a:'Mass and composition determine the structure uniquely (Vogt–Russell).', k:'contested', n:'Called a theorem in older textbooks and never proved. Counterexamples exist among thermally unstable shell-burning configurations. It survives as a working rule because the exceptions are short-lived.'},
  {a:'The star is chemically homogeneous except where burning or convection has changed it.', k:'idealisation', n:'Ignores gravitational settling, radiative levitation, rotational mixing and accretion of processed material. Settling alone changes the surface helium abundance of the Sun by about 10% over its lifetime and must be included to match helioseismology.'},
  {a:'Mass loss follows a one-parameter empirical law.', k:'auxiliary', n:'Reimers or Blöcker with an efficiency η fitted to observed populations. For massive stars the final mass, the supernova type and the black-hole mass function all follow from it, and it is uncertain by factors of two.'},
  {a:'The star is single and has always been single.', k:'idealisation', n:'False for roughly half of massive stars, which exchange mass with a companion at some point. Binary interaction is now understood to dominate the production of stripped-envelope supernovae and of the gravitational-wave black-hole population.'}
 ],
 notation:[
  {s:R`m,\ r(m)`, r:'"the mass coordinate"', m:'Stellar structure is written in Lagrangian form, with the interior mass m as independent variable and the radius r(m) as a dependent one. The reason is that m is conserved as the star expands and contracts, so the grid does not have to move; in Eulerian form every boundary would be a free boundary.', d:'kg; m'},
  {s:R`P,\ \rho`, r:'"pressure", "density"', m:'Total pressure includes gas, radiation and, where relevant, degeneracy terms; radiation pressure is 4% of the total at the centre of the Sun and dominates above about 50 M⊙, which is what limits stellar masses. The central density of the Sun is 1.5×10⁵ kg m⁻³, 150 times water, and the material is still very nearly an ideal gas because it is ionised.', d:'Pa; kg m⁻³'},
  {s:R`L(m),\ \varepsilon_{\rm nuc}`, r:'"the luminosity at mass m"; "epsilon"', m:'L(m) is the energy crossing the sphere enclosing m per unit time, and ε is the generation rate per unit mass. Note the surprise: the Sun\'s ε at its centre is about 17 W per cubic metre, less than a compost heap. Stars are luminous because they are enormous, not because their cores are energetic.', d:'W; W kg⁻¹'},
  {s:R`\nabla_{\rm rad},\ \nabla_{\rm ad}`, r:'"del rad", "del ad"', m:'Logarithmic temperature gradients d ln T/d ln P, the one required to carry the flux radiatively and the one a displaced parcel would follow. The Schwarzschild criterion for convection is ∇_rad > ∇_ad, with ∇_ad = 0.4 for a monatomic ideal gas. It is a clean local stability result that says nothing about the convective flux once instability sets in.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\kappa(\rho,T,X_{i})`, r:'"the opacity"', m:'Cross-section per unit mass for photon absorption and scattering, Rosseland-mean averaged over frequency. It ranges from 0.02 m² kg⁻¹ (electron scattering, the floor) to 10³ near ionisation edges. The most consequential input in a stellar model, entirely tabulated, and the subject of an unexplained laboratory discrepancy since 2015.', d:'m² kg⁻¹'},
  {s:R`\mu,\ \mu_{e}`, r:'"mu"; "mu sub ee"', m:'Mean molecular weight per particle and per free electron, in units of the atomic mass unit. μ = 0.61 for a fully ionised solar mix, 1.34 for neutral; μ_e = 2 for any fully ionised material with equal numbers of protons and neutrons. It enters the luminosity to the fourth power and the Chandrasekhar mass to the inverse square.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`X,\ Y,\ Z`, r:'"ex, why, zed"', m:'Mass fractions of hydrogen, helium and everything else, summing to one. Present-day solar surface values X = 0.7381, Y = 0.2485, Z = 0.0134 on the revised scale, against Z = 0.0169 on the older one. Astronomers call every element beyond helium a metal, which is why "metallicity" includes oxygen and carbon and does not include most metals.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\alpha_{\rm MLT}=\ell_{\rm mix}/H_{P}`, r:'"alpha em ell tee"', m:'The mixing length in units of the pressure scale height H_P = P/ρg, fitted to about 1.5–2.2. This single number sets the radius and effective temperature of every cool star model, and hence every isochrone, and hence every globular-cluster age used to cross-check cosmology. It is the atlas\'s clearest example of a fudge factor bearing a long inferential load.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`M_{\rm Ch}=1.456\,(2/\mu_{e})^{2}M_{\odot}`, r:'"the Chandrasekhar mass"', m:'The maximum mass of a cold star supported by electron degeneracy, assembled from ħ, c, G and the atomic mass unit with no fitted quantity anywhere in it. Observed white dwarfs cluster near 0.6 M⊙ and none exceeds it. It also fixes the energy scale of type Ia supernovae, and hence the cosmological distance ladder.', d:'kg (quoted in M⊙ = 1.989×10³⁰ kg)'},
  {s:R`S(E),\ \ 2\pi\eta=Z_{1}Z_{2}e^{2}/2\varepsilon_{0}\hbar v`, r:'"the astrophysical S-factor"; "the Sommerfeld parameter"', m:'The Coulomb penetrability exp(−2πη) is factored out of the cross-section so that the residual S(E) varies slowly and can be extrapolated downward in energy. Note the notation clash: this η is not the baryon-to-photon ratio of the cosmology sheet and not the mass-loss efficiency of this one. The field reuses the letter three times.', d:'keV barn; dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\Delta\nu,\ \delta\nu`, r:'"the large and small frequency separations"', m:'Δν is the inverse of twice the sound travel time to the centre and measures mean density; δν, the small separation between modes of degree ℓ and ℓ+2, probes the composition gradient in the core and therefore the age. For the Sun, Δν = 135 μHz and δν ≈ 9 μHz. Asteroseismology gets radii to 2% and ages to 10% from these two numbers.', d:'Hz (μHz in practice)'},
  {s:R`L_{\rm Edd}=4\pi GMm_{p}c/\sigma_{T}`, r:'"the Eddington luminosity"', m:'The luminosity at which radiation pressure on free electrons balances gravity on the protons they are bound to electrostatically. σ_T = 6.65×10⁻²⁹ m² is the Thomson cross-section. It is a spherically symmetric bound, not a law: apparently super-Eddington sources exist and are explained by anisotropy or clumping.', d:'W'}
 ],
 worked:{t:'The Sun\'s central temperature from two lines of algebra, and why it is not enough',
  steps:[
   'For a sphere of uniform density, integrating hydrostatic equilibrium gives a central pressure P_c = 3GM²/8πR⁴. With M = 1.989×10³⁰ kg and R = 6.957×10⁸ m this is 1.35×10¹⁴ Pa, about 1.3 billion atmospheres.',
   'Treat the material as an ideal gas with mean molecular weight μ = 0.61 (fully ionised solar composition) and evaluate at the mean density ρ̄ = 1408 kg m⁻³: T_c = P_c μ m_u / (ρ̄ k_B) = 7.0×10⁶ K.',
   'The standard solar model gives 1.57×10⁷ K. The estimate is low by a factor of 2.2, and the reason is the uniform-density assumption: the real central density is 1.5×10⁵ kg m⁻³, about 110 times the mean, so the Sun is far more centrally condensed than the calculation allows.',
   'Take the model value and ask whether fusion is possible. At 1.57×10⁷ K, k_BT = 1.35 keV. The Coulomb barrier between two protons at a separation where the strong force acts, about 2 fm, is e²/4πε₀r ≈ 0.7 MeV — some five hundred times larger. Classically nothing burns.',
   'Tunnelling rescues it, but only for a narrow band of energies: the Gamow peak for the p–p reaction sits near 6 keV, roughly four and a half times k_BT, where the falling Maxwell–Boltzmann tail and the rising penetrability cross. The fraction of pairs in that band is of order 10⁻⁵, and the weak-interaction step makes the first reaction slower still.'],
  a:'7 million kelvin from two lines of algebra against 15.7 million from the full structure equations — right to a factor of two, and useless for anything quantitative, because the nuclear rate depends on temperature roughly as T⁴ for the p–p chain and as T¹⁸ for CNO. A factor of 2.2 in T_c is a factor of 20 in pp luminosity and a factor of 10⁵ in CNO. This is the reason stellar astrophysics is a computational subject rather than an analytic one, and also the reason the solar neutrino problem was so hard to adjudicate: since the ⁸B flux scales as roughly T_c¹⁸, a 5% error in the central temperature would have accounted for the entire factor-of-three deficit. Helioseismology, not stellar theory, is what eventually excluded that escape route.'}
},

plasma: {
 origin:[
  {y:'1923–29', who:'Irving Langmuir & Lewi Tonks, General Electric, Schenectady', t:'A wire in a discharge, and a new word',
   b:'Langmuir, working on gas-filled lamps and vacuum tubes for a company that wanted brighter bulbs, inserts a small biased wire into a mercury-vapour discharge and sweeps its voltage. The resulting current–voltage curve gives the electron temperature from the slope of its exponential region and the density from its saturation — the first quantitative diagnostic of an ionised gas, and still in use. He finds a thin non-neutral layer at every surface, which he calls a sheath, and needs a name for the quasi-neutral bulk beyond it. He takes "plasma" from the blood, which carries corpuscles about; Mott-Smith later recalled the analogy being made explicitly. The electron oscillation at ω_pe follows in the 1929 paper with Tonks.'},
  {y:'1938–46', who:'Anatoly Vlasov; Lev Landau', t:'A damping that should not be there',
   b:'Vlasov drops the collision term and treats the plasma with a self-consistent mean field, then looks for normal modes. He gets a real dispersion relation and no damping. Landau, in 1946, points out that the problem is an initial-value problem, not an eigenvalue problem: Laplace-transform in time, and the contour must be deformed around a pole at the wave phase velocity, whose residue is imaginary. The wave damps. The result was disbelieved for eighteen years, and not unreasonably — the equation has no dissipation in it, conserves entropy exactly, and is time-reversible.'},
  {y:'1942–49', who:'Hannes Alfvén', t:'Waves in a conducting fluid, and a two-page note nobody believed',
   b:'Alfvén publishes a short Nature note arguing that a magnetised conducting fluid carries a transverse wave at B/√(μ₀ρ), the field lines behaving like elastic strings. The reception was frosty; the well-attested story is that the field changed its mind only when Fermi, at a Chicago seminar in 1948, listened and said "of course". Lundquist demonstrated the waves in liquid mercury in 1949. Alfvén\'s frozen-flux theorem, from the same period, became the most-used and — by his own later account — the most-abused result in astrophysics.'},
  {y:'1946–58', who:'Thomson & Blackman; Kurchatov; the ZETA team', t:'The pinch, and a false announcement',
   b:'The obvious way to confine a plasma is to pass a current through it and let its own magnetic field squeeze it. The obvious way fails: the column sausages and kinks within microseconds, as Kruskal and Shafranov explained with an ideal-MHD stability criterion. Britain\'s ZETA announced thermonuclear neutrons in January 1958 to front-page coverage; within months the neutrons were shown to come from beam–target reactions in an unstable, non-thermal plasma, and the announcement was retracted. The episode taught the field to demand a spectroscopic or scattering measurement of temperature rather than infer it from the product.'},
  {y:'1957–64', who:'Sweet; Parker; Petschek', t:'Reconnection, at the wrong rate',
   b:'Sweet and Parker construct the first quantitative model of magnetic field lines breaking and rejoining in a thin resistive layer, and obtain a rate scaling as the inverse square root of the Lundquist number. For the solar corona, with S ≈ 10¹²–10¹⁴, this predicts flares lasting months. Flares last minutes. Petschek proposed a slow-shock geometry in 1964 that gives a fast rate; uniform-resistivity simulations do not reproduce it. The rate problem was not resolved until the plasmoid instability and collisionless Hall physics were understood, and in a sense it is still open, because the observed rate near 0.1 v_A appears to be insensitive to which mechanism supplies it.'},
  {y:'1964–68', who:'John Malmberg & Charles Wharton; Gould & O\'Neil', t:'Landau damping measured, then undone',
   b:'Malmberg and Wharton launch a wave on a collisionless plasma column with one probe and follow its decay with a movable second probe. The damping is there, and its dependence on kλ_D follows Landau\'s formula. Four years later the same group, with Gould and O\'Neil, does something stranger: excite a wave at one wavenumber, let it damp to nothing, excite a second at another wavenumber, and at a calculable later time a third wave appears spontaneously. The damped wave had not been dissipated at all; its information had gone into fine structure in velocity space and could be unwound.'},
  {y:'1968–69', who:'Lev Artsimovich; the Culham laser team', t:'A Russian claim, checked by a British laser',
   b:'At the Novosibirsk IAEA conference Artsimovich reports electron temperatures near 1 keV in the T-3 tokamak, with confinement tens of times better than the Bohm scaling everyone assumed. The claim was not believed, because Soviet temperature estimates were inferred from the plasma conductivity rather than measured. Artsimovich then did something unusual: he invited a British group to bring their own instrument. Peacock, Robinson, Forrest and Wilcock shipped a ruby-laser Thomson scattering system to Moscow, and in 1969 confirmed the temperature — if anything higher than claimed. Within months Princeton had begun converting its Model C stellarator into a tokamak, and the world programme changed direction.'},
  {y:'1974–82', who:'von Goeler, Stodiek & Sauthoff; Wagner and the ASDEX team', t:'Sawteeth and the H-mode',
   b:'Soft X-ray diode arrays on the Princeton ST tokamak reveal that the core temperature rises steadily and then collapses in about a hundred microseconds, over and over — an internal relaxation, invisible from outside, with an inversion radius where the safety factor passes through one. Kadomtsev\'s full-reconnection model gives roughly the right period and the wrong crash time, and measurements showing that the central safety factor stays below one after the crash contradict it outright. Then in 1982 ASDEX finds that above a heating-power threshold the plasma spontaneously doubles its confinement and forms an edge transport barrier. Nobody predicted the H-mode; ITER is designed around it.'}
 ],
 exps:[
  {n:'The Langmuir probe', who:'Irving Langmuir & Harold Mott-Smith, General Electric Research Laboratory', y:'1924–26', fig:'langprobe',
   setup:'A small tungsten wire, of order 0.1 mm in diameter and a few millimetres long, sealed through glass into a low-pressure mercury-vapour or noble-gas discharge, with the rest of its length insulated. The probe bias is swept from tens of volts negative to positive relative to the anode, and the collected current recorded. At strongly negative bias only ions reach it and the current saturates at the ion flux; as the bias rises, the exponentially small fraction of electrons able to overcome the retarding potential grows; above the plasma potential the electrons saturate in turn. The tungsten is flashed to incandescence between sweeps to clean off deposited mercury, because a contaminated surface shifts the characteristic.',
   meas:'A logarithmic plot of electron current against bias is a straight line in the retarding region with slope e/k_BT_e, giving the electron temperature directly; the saturation level gives the density through the thermal flux. Typical laboratory discharges give T_e of 1–10 eV and n_e of 10¹⁵–10¹⁸ m⁻³, to about 10–20%.',
   infer:'From an I–V curve to a temperature requires the electron distribution to be Maxwellian: the straight line in the semi-log plot is the evidence for that assumption and simultaneously depends on it. A distribution with a beam or a depleted tail gives a curved plot, which is how non-Maxwellian plasmas are detected — a rare case of an assumption and its test being the same measurement.',
   assume:'That the probe is small compared with the mean free path and with the plasma dimensions, so that it does not drain the discharge; that the sheath around it is thin compared with the probe radius, so the collecting area is the probe area; that the surface is clean. The uncontrollable one in a magnetised plasma is that the probe perturbs the flux tube it sits in, since transport along the field is fast and across it slow. This is why probe measurements are trusted in unmagnetised discharges and treated with suspicion in tokamak cores, where they would also melt.'},
  {n:'Collisionless damping, and its reversal', who:'John Malmberg & Charles Wharton, later with Gould & O\'Neil, General Atomic, San Diego', y:'1964; echo 1967–68', fig:'malmberg',
   setup:'A quiescent, magnetically confined plasma column roughly a metre long and a few centimetres across, with density around 10¹⁵ m⁻³ and electron temperature of a few electronvolts, made deliberately collisionless — the electron–electron collision time is long compared with the transit time along the column. A small exciter probe launches an electrostatic wave at a fixed frequency of order 100 MHz; a second probe on a movable shaft is drawn along the axis, recording amplitude and phase against distance. Because the frequency is set by the generator and the wavelength is measured, both real and imaginary parts of the wavenumber are obtained from the same trace.',
   meas:'Exponential spatial decay, with the damping length falling steeply as the wave is pushed toward larger kλ_D — the measured dependence follows the exp(−1/2k²λ_D²) factor over more than a decade in damping rate. For the echo, an exciter at k₁ and a second at k₂ downstream produce a spontaneous third wave at k₂ − k₁ at a predicted, calculable distance further downstream.',
   infer:'That a reversible, collisionless equation can produce exponential decay of an observable — and, from the echo, that the decay is phase mixing rather than dissipation. The second inference is the important one and it is close to airtight: if the energy had been thermalised there would be nothing left to re-phase, and the echo appears exactly where the ballistic velocity-space terms predict.',
   assume:'That collisions are genuinely negligible over the observation length. This is the assumption the echo experiment is most sensitive to, and it is the reason the echo is a better probe of collisionality than of anything else: collisions smear the fine velocity-space structure and kill the echo, so the echo amplitude measures how far the reversibility survives. Also assumed: that the column is uniform along its length and that the probes do not themselves damp the wave.'},
  {n:'T-3, and Thomson scattering brought from Culham', who:'Lev Artsimovich, Kurchatov Institute; Nicol Peacock, Derek Robinson, Michael Forrest & Peter Wilcock, Culham', y:'1968–69', fig:'t3tok',
   setup:'T-3 was a toroidal device of major radius 1.0 m and minor radius about 0.12 m, with a toroidal field near 2.5 T produced by copper coils and a plasma current of 60–100 kA driven inductively by a transformer whose secondary is the plasma itself. The British instrument was independent of all of it: a Q-switched ruby laser fired a joule-scale, 20-nanosecond pulse across the plasma, and light Thomson-scattered from free electrons was collected at 90°, dispersed by a spectrometer and detected by photomultipliers. The scattered spectrum is Doppler-broadened by the electron thermal motion, so its width gives T_e and its integrated intensity gives n_e, with no assumption about equilibrium, conductivity or impurity content. The signal is roughly one scattered photon in 10¹⁴, so the hard part is rejecting stray laser light from the vessel walls.',
   meas:'Electron temperature about 1 keV on axis, density around 3×10¹⁹ m⁻³, and an energy confinement time of a few milliseconds — some thirty times the Bohm-diffusion expectation.',
   infer:'From a scattered spectral width to a thermal temperature. The step that is not deductive is that the broadened spectrum reflects a Maxwellian rather than a runaway-electron tail; the spectral shape itself is the check, and a runaway population would show as a distinct wing. That the measurement was made with foreign apparatus by people with no stake in the result is what made the claim credible, and it is a rare and instructive instance of replication being arranged deliberately across a cold-war boundary.',
   assume:'That the scattering is incoherent — valid when the scattering wavevector is large compared with the inverse Debye length, which sets the required collection geometry. That stray light and plasma line radiation have been subtracted, controlled by firing the laser into an evacuated vessel and into a discharge without the laser. The assumption nobody could control was that T-3\'s behaviour would extrapolate, and the scaling has been the field\'s central problem ever since.'},
  {n:'Sawtooth crashes in the tokamak core', who:'Sigurd von Goeler, Wolfgang Stodiek & Neil Sauthoff, Princeton ST tokamak', y:'1974', fig:null,
   setup:'An array of surface-barrier silicon diodes, each viewing the plasma along a different chord through a beryllium window, recording soft X-ray bremsstrahlung from the hot core with microsecond time resolution. Because the emission depends steeply on electron temperature and density, the chord signals form a crude tomographic view of the core. No probe enters the plasma; the diagnostic is entirely passive.',
   meas:'Central chords show a slow rise of order 10 ms followed by a collapse in about 100 μs, repeating indefinitely. Chords outside a well-defined inversion radius show the mirror image: they rise when the centre falls. A precursor oscillation with poloidal mode number m = 1 grows for a few hundred microseconds before each crash.',
   infer:'The core is periodically flattening its temperature profile by an internal rearrangement that conserves energy while redistributing it, with the inversion radius identifying the surface where the safety factor passes through unity. Kadomtsev\'s 1975 model — complete reconnection of the q < 1 region — gives roughly the right period. It does not give the right crash time, and direct measurements of the internal field by polarimetry and motional Stark effect show that the central safety factor stays below one after the crash, which the model says it should not.',
   assume:'That the soft X-ray emissivity tracks temperature in a known way, which requires the impurity content to be steady — the usual control is to compare chords at different photon energies. That the inversion is not an artefact of the chord geometry, controlled by varying the viewing angle. The unresolved item is not an assumption but an admission: fifty years on, there is no accepted model that reproduces both the crash time and the post-crash safety-factor profile, in the best-diagnosed plasma there is.'}
 ],
 assume:[
  {a:'The plasma parameter Λ = nλ_D³ is large, so collective fields dominate binary collisions.', k:'definition', n:'This is what "a plasma" means operationally, not merely ionisation. Λ ≈ 10⁸ in a tokamak and 10⁹ in the solar wind; of order unity in white-dwarf interiors and compressed fusion fuel, where the entire apparatus fails and one needs strongly coupled theory.'},
  {a:'Quasineutrality holds on scales above the Debye length.', k:'idealisation', n:'A consequence of screening, not an extra postulate. It fails in sheaths at every material boundary, which is exactly where every laboratory plasma is measured and every wall is eroded.'},
  {a:'Two-particle correlations can be dropped from the ensemble-averaged Klimontovich equation.', k:'idealisation', n:'The Vlasov closure, with relative error O(1/Λ). Retaining the term to leading order gives the Landau collision operator, which is where irreversibility genuinely enters — a point routinely confused with Landau damping, which lives in the collisionless equation.'},
  {a:'Ideal MHD describes large-scale dynamics even when the collisional ordering used to derive it is violated.', k:'contested', n:'In the corona and in tokamaks the mean free path exceeds the system size by orders of magnitude, and MHD stability limits are nonetheless respected. The usual defence — magnetisation substitutes for collisions, and the energy principle is variational and error-tolerant — is a rationalisation, not a derivation.'},
  {a:'The resistivity may be set to zero (ideal Ohm\'s law).', k:'idealisation', n:'A singular limit: setting η = 0 removes the highest derivative, so the ideal solution is not the limit of resistive solutions. Current sheets thin until the neglected term dominates. This is Prandtl\'s boundary layer with η in place of viscosity.'},
  {a:'The pressure is a scalar.', k:'idealisation', n:'True only for a collisional, isotropic distribution. In a magnetised collisionless plasma the parallel and perpendicular pressures differ, driving firehose and mirror instabilities that are measured routinely in the solar wind and are outside MHD entirely.'},
  {a:'The displacement current is negligible, so ∇×B = μ₀J.', k:'idealisation', n:'Valid when u ≪ c. It is what makes the MHD system non-relativistic and removes light waves from the description; it is also why MHD cannot describe the electromagnetic emission by which most astrophysical plasmas are actually observed.'},
  {a:'Transport coefficients from a local ordering apply to a globally inhomogeneous device.', k:'auxiliary', n:'The gyrokinetic flux-tube approach assumes scale separation between the turbulence and the profile. It fails for large ρ*, which is exactly the small-machine regime from which ITER is extrapolated.'},
  {a:'Confinement scales as the IPB98(y,2) regression across machines.', k:'auxiliary', n:'A log-linear fit over several thousand discharges from about a dozen devices, extrapolated by roughly a factor of two in linear size to ITER. It is a curve fit, not a theory, and the 95% interval on the extrapolation spans about a factor of two in τ_E.'},
  {a:'Plasma-facing surfaces do not alter the bulk.', k:'empirical', n:'False in practice and managed rather than assumed away: wall material sputters in, radiates, and dilutes the fuel. A single tungsten atom per 10⁴ fuel ions is enough to radiate away a reactor\'s power, which is why wall choice is a physics decision and not an engineering one.'}
 ],
 notation:[
  {s:R`f_{s}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{v},t)`, r:'"eff sub ess"', m:'The one-particle distribution function for species s: a density on six-dimensional phase space, normalised so that its velocity integral is the number density. It is not a probability, and it is not the exact microscopic state — it is the ensemble average of the Klimontovich sum of delta functions, and the difference between the two is where collisions come from.', d:'m⁻⁶ s³'},
  {s:R`\lambda_{D}=\sqrt{\varepsilon_{0}k_{B}T_{e}/n_{e}e^{2}}`, r:'"the Debye length"', m:'The distance over which a test charge is screened. In numbers, λ_D = 7430 √(T_e[eV]/n_e[m⁻³]) metres: about 70 μm in a tokamak, 7 m in the solar wind, 0.7 mm in a typical laboratory column. It is the shortest length at which the fluid description can mean anything.', d:'m'},
  {s:R`\Lambda=n\lambda_{D}^{3}`, r:'"the plasma parameter"', m:'The number of particles inside a Debye sphere, which measures how collective the system is. The ratio of the mean potential energy between neighbours to the kinetic energy is of order Λ^{−2/3}, so Λ ≫ 1 is what makes a perturbative expansion in the interaction possible at all. Not to be confused with the Coulomb logarithm ln Λ, which is a different, though related, Λ.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\omega_{pe},\ \Omega_{ci}=eB/m_{i}`, r:'"the plasma frequency"; "the ion cyclotron frequency"', m:'The electron fluid\'s natural oscillation against the ions, f_pe ≈ 8980√(n_e[cm⁻³]) Hz, and the ion gyration rate. The first depends only on density, not temperature — the one plasma quantity with no thermal content; the second only on field and mass. Their ratio orders everything: MHD requires ω ≪ Ω_ci.', d:'rad s⁻¹'},
  {s:R`\rho_{i}=v_{th,i}/\Omega_{ci},\ \ \rho_{*}=\rho_{i}/a`, r:'"the ion gyroradius"; "rho star"', m:'The radius of an ion\'s circular orbit about a field line — a few millimetres in a tokamak, 100 km in the solar wind — and its ratio to the machine size. ρ* is the expansion parameter of gyrokinetics and the quantity that must be matched between a small device and a large one for a dimensionless-similarity experiment to mean anything.', d:'m; dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\beta=2\mu_{0}p/B^{2}`, r:'"beta"', m:'Thermal pressure over magnetic pressure: 10⁻⁴ to 10⁻² in the solar corona, of order 1 in the solar wind, a few percent in a tokamak. It is the economic quantity of magnetic fusion, because fusion power density goes as β²B⁴ and the achievable β is set by MHD stability limits.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`v_{A}=B/\sqrt{\mu_{0}\rho}`, r:'"the Alfvén speed"', m:'The speed of a transverse disturbance travelling along the field, from the tension B²/μ₀ and the inertia ρ — literally the wave speed of an elastic string whose tension is magnetic. About 10⁶ m s⁻¹ in the corona, 50 km s⁻¹ in the solar wind. Fast reconnection rates are always quoted as a fraction of it.', d:'m s⁻¹'},
  {s:R`\eta,\ R_{m}=uL/\eta,\ S=Lv_{A}/\eta`, r:'"the resistivity"; "the magnetic Reynolds and Lundquist numbers"', m:'η here is the magnetic diffusivity, not viscosity and not the Sommerfeld parameter. R_m ≈ 10⁶ in the solar convection zone and 10¹⁰ in the interstellar medium; S ≈ 10¹²–10¹⁴ in the corona. The diffusive term is negligible everywhere and cannot be dropped anywhere, because it is the highest derivative.', d:'m² s⁻¹; dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\gamma,\ \ \varepsilon(k,\omega)=0`, r:'"gamma"; "the dielectric function"', m:'γ is the imaginary part of the frequency: negative for damping, positive for growth. The dispersion relation ε(k,ω) = 0 has a pole at v = ω/k, and the sign of ∂f₀/∂v there fixes the sign of γ. A Maxwellian slopes downward, so waves damp; add a beam and the same formula gives the bump-on-tail instability. Damping and growth are one expression with two signs.', d:'rad s⁻¹'},
  {s:R`q(r)=\frac{rB_{\phi}}{RB_{\theta}}`, r:'"the safety factor"', m:'The number of times a field line goes the long way round a torus per circuit the short way. The Kruskal–Shafranov condition q > 1 at the edge is the limit on plasma current; the surface where q = 1 is where sawtooth crashes originate. It is called the safety factor because exceeding the limit destroys the discharge.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\tau_{E}=W/P_{\rm loss}`, r:'"the energy confinement time"', m:'Stored thermal energy divided by loss power — an operational definition, not a transport coefficient. The Lawson-type ignition criterion is stated as a triple product nTτ_E ≳ 3×10²¹ keV s m⁻³. τ_E is a few milliseconds in T-3, about a second in JET, and projected at several seconds in ITER by regression.', d:'s'},
  {s:R`\mathbf{E},\mathbf{B},\mathbf{u},\ \nabla\!\cdot\!\mathbf{B}=0`, r:'"ee, bee, you"', m:'The MHD state variables. Note the asymmetry: ∇·B = 0 is a constraint on the initial data that the induction equation then preserves exactly, so a numerical scheme that violates it is producing magnetic monopoles, and the artefacts are severe. Half the effort in MHD codes goes into enforcing a condition that is automatic in exact arithmetic.', d:'V m⁻¹; T; m s⁻¹'}
 ],
 worked:{t:'How fast a Langmuir wave damps, and why the answer changes by twenty orders of magnitude',
  steps:[
   'Take a laboratory column with n_e = 10¹⁵ m⁻³ and T_e = 10 eV — close to Malmberg and Wharton\'s conditions. Then λ_D = 7430√(10/10¹⁵) = 0.74 mm, and f_pe = 8980√(10⁹ cm⁻³) = 284 MHz.',
   'The Landau rate is γ/ω_pe = −√(π/8)(kλ_D)⁻³ exp[−1/2(kλ_D)² − 3/2], with √(π/8) = 0.6267.',
   'At kλ_D = 0.1 (wavelength 4.7 cm): the exponent is −50 − 1.5, so the exponential is 4×10⁻²³ and γ/ω_pe ≈ −2×10⁻¹⁹. The wave would survive some 10¹⁸ oscillations. Undetectable.',
   'At kλ_D = 0.3 (wavelength 1.6 cm): the exponent is −5.56 − 1.5, the exponential is 8.6×10⁻⁴, and γ/ω_pe = −0.6267 × 37.0 × 8.6×10⁻⁴ = −0.020. The amplitude falls by e in about eight periods.',
   'At kλ_D = 0.5 (wavelength 9.3 mm): the exponent is −2 − 1.5, the exponential is 0.030, and γ/ω_pe = −0.6267 × 8 × 0.030 = −0.15. The wave is gone within one oscillation and the concept of a wave is barely applicable.',
   'So over a factor of five in wavenumber the damping rate moves by eighteen orders of magnitude, which is why the measurement had to be made by scanning kλ_D rather than by checking a single number — and why an experiment at the wrong wavelength would have seen either nothing or no wave at all.'],
  a:'γ/ω_pe rises from 10⁻¹⁹ to 0.15 as kλ_D goes from 0.1 to 0.5. The physical reading: damping comes from particles moving near the wave phase velocity ω/k, and for kλ_D ≪ 1 that velocity sits far out on the Maxwellian tail where exponentially few particles live. What is remarkable is not the formula but what it describes. The Vlasov equation has no collision term, conserves every functional of f exactly, and runs equally well backwards; the damping it predicts is the destructive interference of a continuum of undamped modes, with the perturbation developing structure in velocity like exp(ikvt) so that any smooth velocity integral — the charge density, hence the field — decays while nothing is lost. The echo experiment recovers it. This is macroscopic irreversible-looking relaxation with no coarse-graining, no ensemble, no molecular chaos and no entropy production, and it has been unwound in a laboratory. Philosophers of statistical mechanics have made almost no use of the case.'}
},

matsci: {
 origin:[
  {y:'1678', who:'Robert Hooke', t:'An anagram, then a law',
   b:'Hooke publishes "ceiiinosssttuv" in 1660 and reveals it eighteen years later as *ut tensio, sic vis* — as the extension, so the force. It is the first constitutive law: a statement not about mechanics in general but about how a particular class of material responds, and the template for everything materials science does. Everything in the subject that is a law of nature came from elsewhere; everything that is characteristic of the subject is a constitutive relation of this kind, measured rather than derived.'},
  {y:'1822', who:'Augustin-Louis Cauchy', t:'Nine numbers at a point',
   b:'Cauchy\'s tetrahedron argument shows that the traction across any internal surface depends linearly on the surface normal, so the entire internal force state at a point is a second-rank tensor — nine components, six after angular-momentum balance. This is a theorem, given the balance laws, and it is the reason continuum mechanics has the shape it does. Navier, Poisson and Cauchy then spent two decades arguing about whether an isotropic solid has one elastic constant or two; the molecular theorists said one, experiment said two, and the continuum formulation won.'},
  {y:'1920–24', who:'A. A. Griffith; Abram Joffé', t:'Strength is about flaws, and about the surface',
   b:'Griffith, at the Royal Aircraft Establishment, draws glass fibres and finds that the thinner they are the stronger, up to about 3.4 GPa against 170 MPa for the bulk — and explains it with an energy balance between released elastic energy and created surface. Independently in Leningrad, Joffé finds that a rock-salt crystal, feeble and brittle in air, becomes both far stronger and ductile when tested immersed in warm water that dissolves its surface as it is loaded. Two experiments, one conclusion: strength is a property of the defect population, particularly at the surface, and not of the bonds.'},
  {y:'1926', who:'Yakov Frenkel', t:'The number that had to be explained away',
   b:'Frenkel estimates the stress needed to slide one atomic plane rigidly over another by modelling the resistance as sinusoidal with the lattice period, and gets τ ≈ G/2π — about 7 GPa for copper. Annealed copper single crystals yield at around 1 MPa. Four orders of magnitude. Very few sciences have so sharp and so early a founding discrepancy, and the whole of dislocation theory exists to close it.'},
  {y:'1934', who:'Egon Orowan; Michael Polanyi; G. I. Taylor', t:'Three people, one unseen line, within months',
   b:'Orowan in Berlin, Polanyi also in Berlin, and Taylor in Cambridge independently propose within the same year that slip proceeds not by rigid translation but by the motion of a line defect, so that only the bonds along the line break at any instant. Orowan and Polanyi knew each other and agreed to publish separately; Taylor had no contact with either. The required stress drops to the Peierls–Nabarro value, which is exponentially small in the core width and lands in the right range. No one had seen a dislocation, and no one would for twenty-two years.'},
  {y:'1950–52', who:'Frank & Read; Herring & Galt', t:'How they multiply, and what happens without them',
   b:'Cold work raises the dislocation density by five orders of magnitude, so the defects must be generated, not merely present. Frank and Read — the story is that they arrived at the same mechanism independently at the same conference and wrote it up together — describe a pinned segment bowing out under stress and pinching off loop after loop. Herring and Galt take the converse route: micrometre-diameter tin whiskers, grown essentially dislocation-free, bend elastically to strains of a couple of percent, stresses of order a gigapascal, against about 10 MPa for bulk tin. Remove the defects and the discrepancy disappears.'},
  {y:'1956', who:'Peter Hirsch, Robert Horne & Michael Whelan; Walter Bollmann', t:'Twenty-two years later, the pictures',
   b:'At the Cavendish, aluminium foils are beaten and electropolished to about a tenth of a micrometre — thin enough for 100 kV electrons — and examined in a Siemens Elmiskop under two-beam diffraction conditions, where the strain field of a dislocation tilts the lattice locally and changes the diffracted intensity, so the defect appears as a dark line. Beam heating supplies the stress, and the team films the lines moving, piling up at boundaries, and multiplying from Frank–Read sources. Bollmann obtained comparable images of stainless steel independently. The entity had been quantitatively correct for two decades before anybody saw one.'},
  {y:'1970', who:'Larry Kaufman & Harold Bernstein', t:'Phase diagrams by fitting, and calling it calculation',
   b:'*Computer Calculation of Phase Diagrams* launches CALPHAD: represent every phase\'s Gibbs energy as a polynomial with coefficients fitted to calorimetry and measured phase boundaries, then compute multicomponent equilibria by minimising the total. It works, and it transformed alloy design. It is also an assessed interpolation constrained by thermodynamics rather than a computation from below, and the word "calculated" in the title has been doing quiet rhetorical work ever since.'}
 ],
 exps:[
  {n:'The Joffé effect', who:'Abram Joffé, M. V. Kirpitschewa & M. A. Lewitsky, Leningrad', y:'1924', fig:null,
   setup:'Cleaved rock-salt crystals a few millimetres across are loaded in simple tension. The whole experiment is the comparison of two conditions: the same crystal tested in air, and tested while immersed in warm water which continuously dissolves its surface during loading. The dissolution rate has to be slow enough that the specimen is not consumed and fast enough to remove the surface layer as the load builds, so the water temperature is the control variable.',
   meas:'In air the crystals fail in a brittle manner at a few megapascals. Under dissolving water the reported strengths rise by two to three orders of magnitude, into the gigapascal range, and the crystals deform plastically before failing rather than shattering.',
   infer:'That the weakness resides in surface flaws rather than in the bonds, and that removing the surface removes the flaws. The inference is not tight: later work showed that part of the effect is a change in surface condition and dislocation nucleation rather than crack removal alone, and the size of the reported enhancement has been disputed. But the qualitative conclusion — that a brittle solid can be made strong and ductile by treating its surface, without changing anything inside — survived.',
   assume:'That the water acts only by dissolving, and not by lubricating, by altering the surface charge, or by blunting cracks through a chemical effect at the tip. This is the assumption that could not be controlled in 1924 and that later criticism attacked. The experiment nevertheless did the historically decisive work: together with Griffith it made "strength is a defect property" the default position four years before anyone knew what the defects were.'},
  {n:'Griffith\'s glass fibres and cracked bulbs', who:'A. A. Griffith, Royal Aircraft Establishment, Farnborough', y:'1920', fig:'griffith',
   setup:'Two experiments. First, fibres of English hard glass drawn from a heated rod by hand, in diameters from about 1 mm down to 3 μm, measured on a microscope and pulled to failure within hours of drawing — freshly drawn fibres had to be used, because handling and atmospheric attack degrade them within a day. Second, thin-walled spherical and cylindrical glass bulbs into which cracks of measured length were introduced with a glass-cutter\'s wheel, then burst by internal air pressure so that the stress at fracture could be computed from the geometry. He also measured the surface energy of glass separately, by hanging fibres at 730 °C and finding the load at which surface tension neither contracted nor extended them.',
   meas:'Fibre strength rises from about 170 MPa at millimetre diameter to about 3.4 GPa at 3 μm, with an extrapolation toward roughly 11 GPa at zero diameter, close to the estimated molecular cohesion. For the cracked bulbs, the product of fracture stress and the square root of crack length was constant to about 10%.',
   infer:'From σ√a = constant to an energy criterion: a crack extends when the elastic energy released exceeds the surface energy created, giving σ_f = √(2Eγ_s/πa). The step that is not deductive is the identification of the measured constant with 2Eγ_s/π using an independently measured surface energy — and it worked, for glass, to within the experimental scatter.',
   assume:'That the fibres contain flaws whose size scales with diameter, which is the reason for the size effect and was not independently verified; that the introduced cracks are sharp and of the measured length; that fracture is purely elastic with no plastic work. The last is true for glass and catastrophically false for metals, where the plastic work at the crack tip exceeds the surface energy by factors of 10² to 10⁴. Irwin and Orowan repaired this by replacing γ_s with a measured effective toughness, which preserves Griffith\'s scaling and abandons his constant.'},
  {n:'Whiskers and micropillars: the control experiment', who:'Conyers Herring & John Galt, Bell Labs; Uchic, Dimiduk and others from 2004', y:'1952; 2004–', fig:null,
   setup:'Tin whiskers of one to two micrometres diameter grow spontaneously from electroplated surfaces and are essentially free of mobile dislocations. Herring and Galt mounted individual whiskers and bent them, measuring the deflection optically and the applied force from a calibrated fibre, so that the elastic strain could be read directly from the curvature. The modern version uses a focused ion beam to machine a cylindrical pillar 0.1–10 μm across out of a bulk single crystal, then compresses it with a flat-punch nanoindenter while recording load and displacement at nanometre resolution.',
   meas:'Whiskers sustain elastic strains of order 2%, which for tin corresponds to stresses near a gigapascal, against roughly 10 MPa for bulk tin — two orders of magnitude. Micropillars show a systematic smaller-is-stronger dependence, roughly a power law with exponent near −0.6 over two decades in diameter, and their stress–strain curves are not smooth but a staircase of discrete strain bursts.',
   infer:'That the weakness of bulk crystals is caused by the defects they contain, since specimens without them approach the theoretical strength. This is the control that the 1934 hypothesis needed, and it is unusually clean: the hypothesis says remove the dislocations and the discrepancy goes, and it does. The strain bursts additionally show that plastic flow is intermittent and avalanche-like at this scale, which the smooth continuum stress–strain curve averages away.',
   assume:'That the whisker is genuinely dislocation-free, inferred from its growth mechanism rather than observed. For micropillars, that focused-ion-beam milling has not implanted gallium or created a damaged surface layer that changes the result — a real and contested worry, addressed by comparing with pillars made by lithography and etching, which show the same size effect with different absolute values. Whether the size effect is dislocation starvation or source truncation is still argued.'},
  {n:'Direct observation of dislocations in motion', who:'Peter Hirsch, Robert Horne & Michael Whelan, Cavendish Laboratory', y:'1956', fig:'tem56',
   setup:'Aluminium is beaten to foil and then thinned electrolytically to roughly 100–500 nm, the upper limit set by the mean free path of 100 kV electrons in aluminium. The foil goes into a Siemens Elmiskop I and is tilted until a single strong diffracted beam is excited — the two-beam condition. The objective aperture then selects either the transmitted or the diffracted beam to form the image, so that any local lattice rotation changes the intensity. A dislocation strain field rotates the planes by a fraction of a degree over a few tens of nanometres, which is enough to produce a dark line of high contrast against the surrounding foil. The specimen is stressed by the thermal gradients of the beam itself, and the screen is filmed with a cine camera.',
   meas:'Dark lines of width 10–20 nm, visible individually, moving at speeds resolvable at cine frame rates, piling up against grain boundaries, intersecting to form jogs, and emitting successive loops from pinned segments in the geometry Frank and Read had described in 1950. Areal densities of 10¹⁰ m⁻² in annealed material rising to 10¹⁵ m⁻² after cold work.',
   infer:'From a contrast feature to a lattice defect of specific geometry. The identification is made quantitatively by the g·b invisibility criterion: a dislocation is invisible when the diffraction vector is perpendicular to its Burgers vector, so imaging the same defect under several diffraction conditions and recording when it vanishes determines b. That is what converts "there are dark lines" into "these are dislocations with b = a/2⟨110⟩".',
   assume:'That the thin foil behaves like the bulk. It does not entirely: surfaces are free, so image forces pull dislocations out, and the observed configurations are relaxed relative to bulk ones. Thinning may also introduce defects. The controls are to compare foils of different thickness and to compare densities with independent bulk measurements from X-ray line broadening and etch pitting. The assumption that could not be controlled was that the beam-induced stress drives the same mechanisms as an applied load.'},
  {n:'The uniaxial tensile test', who:'Standardised as ASTM E8/E8M and ISO 6892; in routine use since the nineteenth century', y:'—', fig:'tensile',
   setup:'A specimen of prescribed geometry — commonly 12.5 mm diameter over a 50 mm parallel gauge length, with generous shoulder radii and threaded or shouldered grips designed so that failure occurs in the gauge section and not at the grips — is pulled in a screw-driven or servo-hydraulic frame at a controlled crosshead rate. Load is measured by a strain-gauge load cell in series with the specimen. Extension is measured not from the crosshead, which includes machine compliance, but by a clip-on extensometer whose knife edges sit directly on the gauge length and which resolves of order a micrometre over 25 mm, that is a strain of 4×10⁻⁵.',
   meas:'A load–extension trace converted to engineering stress and strain by dividing by the original area and the original gauge length. Reported: Young\'s modulus from the initial slope; the 0.2% offset proof stress; the ultimate tensile strength, which is the maximum of the engineering curve; the elongation and reduction of area at fracture.',
   infer:'Almost every reported quantity is a convention rather than a measured property of the material. The ultimate tensile strength is the point at which necking begins, and it exists only because the engineering stress uses the original area — the true stress rises monotonically to fracture. Yield strength for a material without a sharp yield point is defined by a 0.2% offset, a number chosen by committee. Elongation at fracture depends on the gauge length, because necking localises the deformation, which is why the gauge length must be quoted with the value.',
   assume:'That the stress state is uniaxial and uniform over the gauge section — true until necking, false afterwards, which is exactly when the headline number is read off. That the specimen represents the material, which requires the gauge section to contain enough grains to constitute a representative volume; for a coarse-grained casting or an additively manufactured part with columnar grains it may not, and then the result is a property of the specimen. This is operationalism as engineering practice: the standards exist because the quantities do not exist independently of the protocol, and the field says so in the standard itself.'}
 ],
 assume:[
  {a:'A continuum limit exists: fields are defined on volumes small against the body and large against the lattice.', k:'idealisation', n:'The limit does not exist mathematically and is a physical stipulation. It fails in films a few atoms thick, at crack tips where the stress field diverges, and in nanocrystalline metal where grain size approaches dislocation core size.'},
  {a:'A representative volume element exists, containing enough microstructure to be size-independent.', k:'idealisation', n:'Needs 10²–10³ grains, so tens to hundreds of micrometres for a polycrystal. Stent struts, MEMS beams and additively manufactured parts routinely violate it, and then "the yield strength of this alloy" is not a well-defined quantity.'},
  {a:'Stress depends only on the local history of deformation (local action and determinism).', k:'definition', n:'Part of the Truesdell–Noll constitutive framework. It excludes non-local effects that are demonstrably present at small scale — strain-gradient plasticity exists precisely because the local assumption fails when the gradient length approaches the microstructural length.'},
  {a:'Material response is frame-indifferent.', k:'contested', n:'Unchallenged as engineering practice. Müller and others observed that kinetic-theory derivations of transport coefficients produce small frame-dependent terms, so whether it is a principle of physics or a modelling convention is genuinely open.'},
  {a:'Elastic strains are small and the response is linear.', k:'idealisation', n:'The first term of a Taylor expansion of the interatomic potential about equilibrium. Accurate to a few tenths of a percent strain in metals — that is, for the whole elastic range and nothing beyond it. Rubber and biological tissue need finite-strain measures from the start.'},
  {a:'Plastic flow is smooth and rate-independent below about 0.4 of the melting temperature.', k:'idealisation', n:'Nanoindentation and micropillar tests show plastic flow is a sequence of avalanches with a power-law size distribution. Smoothness is what averaging over a large specimen produces, not what the mechanism does.'},
  {a:'Strengthening mechanisms superpose additively.', k:'auxiliary', n:'Grain refinement, solid solution, precipitation and work hardening are summed, sometimes in quadrature, with no derivation for either rule. It is an engineering convenience that works to perhaps 20% and is used well beyond that.'},
  {a:'Phase diagrams describe the material.', k:'idealisation', n:'They describe equilibrium, and almost every useful engineering alloy is deliberately metastable — martensite, precipitation-hardened aluminium, every glass. The diagram gives the driving force; the kinetics give the material.'},
  {a:'Laboratory strain rates and times extrapolate to service.', k:'contested', n:'Creep data are taken over thousands of hours and extrapolated to design lives of 10⁵ hours by Larson–Miller parameters; fatigue data to 10⁷ cycles were extrapolated to infinity until ultrasonic testing to 10¹⁰ cycles found failures below the supposed endurance limit and abolished it.'},
  {a:'CALPHAD assessments extrapolate outside the composition range where they were fitted.', k:'contested', n:'The high-entropy alloy programme after 2004 relied on it and found that most equimolar five-component compositions predicted to be single-phase solid solutions are in fact multiphase. The framework enforces consistency among measurements; outside them it is interpolation with no data.'}
 ],
 notation:[
  {s:R`\sigma_{ij},\ \mathbf{t}(\mathbf{n})=\sigma\mathbf{n}`, r:'"sigma eye jay"; "the traction"', m:'The Cauchy stress tensor: σ_ij is the i-component of force per unit area on a surface whose normal is j. Symmetric, from angular-momentum balance, so six independent components. Positive in tension by the solid-mechanics convention, which is the opposite of the geomechanics convention, and the sign error is a rite of passage.', d:'Pa'},
  {s:R`\varepsilon_{ij}=\tfrac12(u_{i,j}+u_{j,i})`, r:'"epsilon eye jay"', m:'Small-strain tensor, the symmetrised gradient of the displacement field u. Symmetrising discards the rotational part, which is why a rigid rotation produces no stress — and why the measure fails for large rotations, where the Green–Lagrange strain is needed instead. Engineering shear strain γ = 2ε₁₂ is a factor of two different and the source of endless confusion.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`C_{ijkl};\ E,\ G,\ \nu`, r:'"the stiffness tensor"; "Young\'s modulus, shear modulus, Poisson\'s ratio"', m:'Fourth-rank, with 21 independent components for a triclinic crystal, three for cubic, two for an isotropic body. For copper E = 110–130 GPa depending on direction, G = 48 GPa, ν = 0.34. Note ν here is Poisson\'s ratio and not the frequency of the astrophysics sheets.', d:'Pa; Pa; dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\mathbf{F}=\partial\mathbf{x}/\partial\mathbf{X},\ \mathcal{F}[\mathbf{QF}]=\mathbf{Q}\mathcal{F}[\mathbf{F}]\mathbf{Q}^{T}`, r:'"the deformation gradient"; "the frame-indifference condition"', m:'F maps material line elements to spatial ones and is the fundamental kinematic object of finite deformation; its determinant is the volume ratio. The second expression is material frame-indifference: superposing a rigid rotation Q on the motion must rotate the stress and change nothing else. It eliminates most naively written constitutive models.', d:'dimensionless'},
  {s:R`\mathbf{b}`, r:'"the Burgers vector"', m:'The closure failure of a circuit drawn around a dislocation line, equal to a lattice translation and about 0.25 nm in a typical metal. It is the defect\'s conserved charge: b is the same everywhere along the line, which is why a dislocation cannot simply end inside a crystal but must close on itself or reach a surface. Its orientation relative to the line distinguishes edge from screw.', d:'m'},
  {s:R`\rho_{\rm disl}`, r:'"the dislocation density"', m:'Total dislocation line length per unit volume, from 10¹⁰ m⁻² in annealed metal to 10¹⁵ m⁻² after heavy cold work — the latter being 10⁹ km of line in a cubic centimetre. It is measured by TEM counting or X-ray line broadening, and it is an input to the Taylor hardening relation, not an output of it.', d:'m⁻²'},
  {s:R`\tau_{\rm th}\approx G/2\pi,\ \ \tau_{y},\ \sigma_{y}=\sigma_{0}+k_{y}d^{-1/2}`, r:'"the theoretical shear strength"; "the yield stress"; "the Hall–Petch relation"', m:'The gap between τ_th and τ_y is the founding fact of the subject. Note the distinction between resolved shear stress on a slip system and the tensile yield stress of a polycrystal, which differ by the Taylor factor, about 3.06 for face-centred cubic. k_y runs from about 0.1 MPa·m^{1/2} in aluminium to 0.6 in low-carbon steel.', d:'Pa; Pa·m^{1/2} for k_y'},
  {s:R`K_{I}=Y\sigma\sqrt{\pi a},\ \ K_{IC}`, r:'"the mode-one stress intensity factor"; "kay one see"', m:'K_I characterises the whole crack-tip stress field, which diverges as r^{−1/2}; Y is a geometry factor of order one. K_IC is the critical value at which the crack runs: 0.7 MPa√m for window glass, 20–40 for aerospace aluminium, 50–150 for structural steel. Note the units, which are peculiar and are a consequence of the square-root singularity.', d:'Pa·m^{1/2}'},
  {s:R`\gamma_{s},\ \gamma_{\rm eff}`, r:'"the surface energy"; "the effective fracture energy"', m:'γ_s is about 1 J m⁻² for a solid; γ_eff is what actually appears in the fracture criterion for a metal, and it is 10² to 10⁴ times larger because of plastic work in the crack-tip zone. The substitution is Irwin and Orowan\'s, and it preserves Griffith\'s scaling while replacing his derived constant with a measured one.', d:'J m⁻²'},
  {s:R`da/dN=C(\Delta K)^{m}`, r:'"the Paris law"', m:'Fatigue crack growth per cycle as a power of the stress-intensity range, with m ≈ 3 for steels and 3–4 for aluminium alloys. C and m are fitted, not derived. Integrating this expression from an assumed initial flaw size to the critical one is how aircraft inspection intervals are set, which is worth stating plainly.', d:'m per cycle; C in mixed units'},
  {s:R`G^{\phi}=\sum_{i}x_{i}{}^{\circ}G_{i}^{\phi}+RT\sum_{i}x_{i}\ln x_{i}+\sum_{i<j}x_{i}x_{j}\sum_{\nu}L_{ij}^{\nu}(x_{i}-x_{j})^{\nu}`, r:'"the CALPHAD Gibbs energy"', m:'Reference, ideal-mixing and Redlich–Kister excess terms for one phase. The L coefficients are fitted to calorimetry and measured phase boundaries; the minimisation over phases that follows is exact. The thermodynamics is genuine and the input functions are assessments, and describing the output as "calculated" blurs the two.', d:'J mol⁻¹'},
  {s:R`F=C-P+2,\ \ \mu_{i}^{\alpha}=\mu_{i}^{\beta}`, r:'"the Gibbs phase rule"; "equality of chemical potentials"', m:'Degrees of freedom equal components minus phases plus two. It constrains what a phase diagram can look like — a triple point in a one-component system is a point and not a line — and is a genuine theorem of equilibrium thermodynamics. It says nothing about which phases a real alloy will contain after quenching, which is the question that matters.', d:'dimensionless; J mol⁻¹'}
 ],
 worked:{t:'Four orders of magnitude, and the fitted constant that reproduces them',
  steps:[
   'Frenkel\'s estimate for copper: the shear modulus on the {111}⟨110⟩ slip system is about 48 GPa, so the stress to slide one close-packed plane rigidly over another is τ_th ≈ G/2π = 7.6 GPa.',
   'Measured critically resolved shear stress for a well-annealed copper single crystal: roughly 0.5–1 MPa. The ratio is about 10⁴. This is the discrepancy that dislocation theory was invented to remove.',
   'The Taylor relation says a moving dislocation is impeded mainly by the elastic fields of the others: τ = αGb√ρ, with b = 0.256 nm and α ≈ 0.3 fitted from experiment.',
   'Annealed copper has ρ ≈ 10¹⁰ m⁻², so √ρ = 10⁵ m⁻¹ and τ = 0.3 × 48×10⁹ × 0.256×10⁻⁹ × 10⁵ = 0.37 MPa. Right at the bottom of the measured annealed range.',
   'Heavily cold-worked copper has ρ ≈ 10¹⁵ m⁻², so √ρ = 3.16×10⁷ m⁻¹ and τ = 117 MPa. Converted to a tensile yield stress with the Taylor factor 3.06, that is about 360 MPa, which is what a hard-drawn copper wire does.',
   'So one expression with one fitted constant covers a factor of 300 in strength, from the softest to the hardest state of the same pure metal.'],
  a:'τ = αGb√ρ reproduces annealed and cold-worked copper within the experimental scatter, across two and a half orders of magnitude. Now note carefully what has and has not been achieved. The mechanism is understood: strength comes from dislocations obstructing each other, and the √ρ dependence follows from the mean spacing between them scaling as ρ^{−1/2} and the interaction stress as Gb over that spacing. But α is fitted, and ρ is an input — measured by transmission electron microscopy or X-ray line broadening on the very specimen whose strength is being predicted. Nothing here derives a yield strength from composition and processing, which is what an engineer actually wants and what no theory supplies. This is the pattern throughout the subject and it is worth naming: materials science routinely explains a scaling and measures the constant, and the resulting structure is neither a derivation from below nor mere curve-fitting, but something in between for which the philosophy of science has no settled name.'}
}

};
